{"name":"Harlequin Green","hex":"#46cb18","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"harlequin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pantomime fool, typically dressed in colorful checkered clothes, used as a stock character in commedia dell'arte and other genres.","A greenish-chartreuse color."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Brightly colored, especially in a pattern like that of a harlequin clown's clothes.","Of a greenish-chartreuse color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/harlequin"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Harlequin","blocks":[{"text":"Harlequin is the best-known of the comic servant characters (Zanni) from the Italian commedia dell'arte, associated with the city of Bergamo. The role is traditionally believed to have been introduced by the Italian actor-manager Zan Ganassa in the late 16th century. It was definitively popularized by the Italian actor Tristano Martinelli in Paris in 1584–1585, and became a stock character after Martinelli's death in 1630."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/SAND_Maurice_Masques_et_bouffons_01.jpg/330px-SAND_Maurice_Masques_et_bouffons_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlequin","wikidata":"Q17298"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg/500px-A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg/1280px-A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg/500px-A_scene_from_the_commedia_dell%27_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A scene from the commedia dell'arte played in France before a noble audience in 1571 or 1572 (Museum of Bayeux). Pantalone is front and center, while just to the right and slightly behind is Harlequin in motley costume, \"the oldest known version of Harlequin's costume\".[13][14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_scene_from_the_commedia_dell'_arte_played_in_France_before_a_noble_audience_-_Ferrone_2014_fig34.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Compositions_de_rh%C3%A9torique_de_Mr._Don_Arlequin%2C_1601%2C_p01_%28title_page_Arlechin%29_-_Gallica_2010_%28adjusted%29.jpg/500px-Compositions_de_rh%C3%A9torique_de_Mr._Don_Arlequin%2C_1601%2C_p01_%28title_page_Arlechin%29_-_Gallica_2010_%28adjusted%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Compositions_de_rh%C3%A9torique_de_Mr._Don_Arlequin%2C_1601%2C_p01_%28title_page_Arlechin%29_-_Gallica_2010_%28adjusted%29.jpg/500px-Compositions_de_rh%C3%A9torique_de_Mr._Don_Arlequin%2C_1601%2C_p01_%28title_page_Arlechin%29_-_Gallica_2010_%28adjusted%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tristano Martinelli's Harlequin costume as depicted in his Compositions de rhétorique, 1601","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComposit